import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class TokenTop3 {
  public static List<String> top3(List<String> lines) {
    Map<String, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();

    for (String line : lines) {
      for (String token : line.split("\\W+")) {
        if (token.isEmpty()) continue;
        String key = token.toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);
        freq.put(key, freq.getOrDefault(key, 0) + 1);
      }
    }

    List<Map.Entry<String, Integer>> list = new ArrayList<>(freq.entrySet());
    list.sort((a, b) -> b.getValue() - a.getValue());

    return list.stream()
        .limit(3)
        .map(e -> e.getKey() + ":" + e.getValue())
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    List<String> lines = Arrays.asList(
        "Hello, hello world!",
        "world of HELLO",
        "a-b a_b"
    );
    System.out.println(top3(lines));
  }
}


Questions (answer in order)
1) What does top3 compute, precisely? (Be specific about tokenization rules.)
Tokenization is by regex \\W+ (one-or-more non-word chars). In Java “word” is [A-Za-z0-9_], so underscores and digits are kept inside tokens (e.g., a_b stays one token).
Lowercasing uses Locale.ROOT (good: avoids Turkish-i type issues).

2)What does main print? If the exact output isn’t deterministic, explain what set of outputs are possible and why.
Tokens per line:
"Hello, hello world!" → hello, hello, world
"world of HELLO" → world, of, hello
"a-b a_b" → a, b, a_b
Frequencies:
hello = 3
world = 2
of = 1, a = 1, b = 1, a_b = 1
So the first two outputs are deterministic: hello:3, world:2.
The 3rd element is NOT deterministic because:
Ties are only sorted by value (b.getValue() - a.getValue()), so all =1 entries compare as 0.
The initial iteration order comes from HashMap.entrySet() which is unspecified, so the “first” among ties can vary.
Therefore, main prints one of:
[hello:3, world:2, of:1]
[hello:3, world:2, a:1]
[hello:3, world:2, b:1]
[hello:3, world:2, a_b:1]

3)What are the time and space complexities in terms of:
N = total characters across all strings
U = number of unique tokens
Time: building tokens + map is O(N) expected (amortized O(1) hash updates), plus sorting O(U log U), 
so total O(N + U log U).
Space: map + list store U entries → O(U) (plus temporary arrays from split, but asymptotically still dominated by U 
or N depending how you count transient allocations).

4)Identify two potential issues/bugs (correctness, determinism, performance, or API usage).
lines.size() < 3 is not a bug: .limit(3) will just return fewer elements.
freq is never null, and if it has <3 entries that’s also fine (returns fewer).
Two better issues to cite:
Nondeterministic output for ties (explained above).
Comparator b.getValue() - a.getValue() can overflow in general; safer is Integer.compare(b.getValue(), a.getValue()). 
(Also performance note: split("\\W+") compiles a regex each call; could precompile a Pattern.)

5)Suggest one improvement that keeps behavior the same but makes it more robust for production.
Don’t return null for “<3 results”; return the available results (empty list if none). 
Returning null is usually a worse API.
Make tie-breaking deterministic, e.g. sort by frequency desc, then token asc.

6) How would you change the sort line to (a) avoid overflow and 
(b) make ties deterministic by alphabetical token order? Please write just that comparator.
list.sort((a, b) -> {
  int c = Integer.compare(b.getValue(), a.getValue()); // desc
  if (c != 0) return c;
  return a.getKey().compareTo(b.getKey());             // asc tie-break
});
OR
list.sort(
    Comparator.<Map.Entry<String,Integer>>comparingInt(e -> e.getValue()).reversed()
        .thenComparing(Map.Entry::getKey)
);

7) if you wanted top 3 without sorting all U entries, what data structure / approach would you use and what would the complexity become?
Maintain the best 3 entries seen so far in a min-heap ordered by:
frequency ascending (so the “worst” of the top-3 is at the root)
tie-break deterministically (e.g., reverse key order in the heap so popping removes the least desired one)
For each (token, count):
push into heap
if heap size > 3, pop one
At the end, heap has top 3; pop to list and reverse/sort for final output order.
Complexity
Building frequency map: O(N)
Heap maintenance over U unique tokens: O(U log k) = O(U) since k=3
Total: O(N + U log 3) ≈ O(N + U)
Space: O(U) for the map + O(k) for heap.
(Alternative: quickselect/partial selection, but heap is simplest and very common in interviews.)
